--- body ---
## Context

Design note: `design/2026-11-09-context-length-strategy.md`, reviewed by two
advisors. Do not start before the author has accepted it.

The harness sets limits on context length (230K for workers and unattended
runs; 450K or 550K for interface sessions, by model). Neither a session, nor
the author, nor a committed script can tell whether a session respects its
own. An ad hoc scan of main-session transcripts on padme found, for turns
since 2026-11-01: 281 sessions; 24 with 30 or more turns at or above 230K
cache-read tokens (19 on Opus 5.5, 5 on Fable 5.1); 4 at or above 450K; 1 at
or above 550K. The author asked for up to three small pain points to fix
first. The wider strategy is in the note and is out of scope here.

## Pain points, in order

1. **The author cannot read the ceiling he set.** `scripts/statusline.sh`
   prints a percentage of the window: on a 1M window, 23% at 230K and 55% at
   550K. Print tokens, for example `212K`. No ceiling and no role: the
   author knows both.
2. **No committed command says which sessions overran.**
   `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py` thresholds on cache reads (line 126),
   labels a session by its first skill (line 142), keeps a whole transcript
   when its last record is in the window (line 246), and reports runs. The
   scan above is not reproducible.
3. **A session cannot read its own length.** A probe on 2026-11-09 did it in
   one command: the session id in the environment locates the transcript,
   and the last main-chain assistant record's usage gave 211,485 tokens of
   prompt (209,362 cache read). The transcript format is internal to the
   runtime; the audit already parses it. Both reviewers would diagnose
   before building this one; the author may cut it.

## Author decisions (2026-11-10)

1. The design note is accepted for pain points 1 and 2.
2. "Context length" means the whole prompt (input, cache read, and cache
   creation), not cache reads only, in every tool; each tool says so where it
   prints a figure.
3. The audit's per-model thresholds are read from the route skill's data
   (`skills/route/`), one source.
4. Pain point 3 (a command for a session to read its own length) and the two
   exit criteria that depend on it (that command; raid's wave loop and lair's
   metrics calling it) are cut from this ticket, to be revisited once points
   1 and 2 show whether overruns persist.

## Decisions for the plan

- One definition of "context length" for the status line, the audit, and
  the command: the whole prompt, or cache reads as in the June audit. State
  it where each prints a figure.
- The audit's threshold per model: read the ceilings from the route skill's
  data, or take them as options.
- Each point may land in its own merge request: propose the split at
  planning (`skills/hunt/references/decomposition.md`).

Out of scope, waiting on the author: whether an attended raid is an
interface session; what an interface session may do at its ceiling.

## Test

- Status line, on a fixture input: the token figure for a small and a large
  context; an input with no usage field.
- Audit, on fixture transcripts: a turn outside the window is not counted; a
  session with two runs counts once as a session; the main model is reported.
- Usage reader, on fixture transcripts: the last main-chain assistant
  record; a transcript with no such record; a missing transcript.

## Exit criteria

- [x] The status line shows the context in tokens
- [x] The audit counts sessions as well as runs, restricts events to its
  window, reports the main model, and takes a threshold per model; one
  command reproduces the table of section 1 of the design note
- ~~[ ] One command prints the current session's context in tokens on Claude
  Code, and answers "could not look" with its own exit code on a runtime or
  session it cannot read~~ Cut by the author, 2026-11-10; not met.
- ~~[ ] Raid's wave loop and lair's metrics call that command where they now
  estimate or say nothing~~ Cut by the author, 2026-11-10; not met.
- [x] `make check` passes
